Z||000000||KRMFiero||03-17-2001||11:46 AM||||Okay, my 84 has no power door locks, and no power windows. I would like to have both, I looked into installing power windows and locks but that would be hard to do. If i found a pair of doors in a junk yard with Power windows and locks What would i have to do to wire these things up? (I would also take the Powerwindow switches...)  Where do the power window switch wires run?     Anyone have a spare set of doors with P/W, P/L?<P>also where does the power mirror switch mount? where are the wire harnest for this? how many wires are in it?<P>this would probley be alot easyer then pulling the doors all apart and stuff.<P>Kyle<P>------------------<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE (Red)<BR>1985 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE (White)<P>      <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/~Physcokyle/sig2.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by KRMFiero (edited 03-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||gabriel||03-17-2001||02:51 PM||||&gt;also where does the power mirror switch mount? where are the wire harnest for this? how many wires are in it?<P>The mirror control switch is on the center console below the shifter, in between the power window switches.  I'm installing power mirrors now.<BR>I already have the power locks & windows.<BR>I'll try to post a pic of the setup.<BR>You have to remove the wire harness from the donor, of course. It runs under the console & out to either door. <P>Personally, I think that retrofitting <BR>PW & PL isn't at all easy.<P>  I would definately find the doors w/the correct setup & use my skins.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rodrv6||03-17-2001||02:52 PM||||A number of years ago I added a set of power window doors to a non-power car (both 84's). The power window harness is separate from the main harness.  You will need to remove the console and dash in order to get to it.  The window harness runs along the front just under the bottom of the windshield and down under the console to the switches.  It ties into the main harness through a junction block that is located on the left side of the car under the dash at the base of the windshield pillar on the drivers side.  If your car doesn't have power windows or locks then this junction block may not be installed.  I think the power lock and power mirror harnesses are run in a similar fashion, but I don't know for sure.  The power mirror switch is located on the plate behind the gear shift lever between the power window switches.  Your best bet would probably be to have the donor car handy so you can see what you need.  I know the door locks also use a relay that lives under the dash on the right side of the car.  It's somewhat of a big project, but definatly doable <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||03-17-2001||05:10 PM||||Something you guys need to remember about the mill & drill operation. Each Fiero door was cut for its own specific space frame. Meaning, sometimes, not always, a pair of Fiero doors just will not align properly to a different frame, like when you put different doors on and just change skins. Many times if they do hook up, it is literally impossibly to get them lined up as perfectly as the factory did, and that makes an even bigger mess since the Fiero door does not have glass surround.  At the factory, the doors and frame had the holes drilled, then the doors separated from the space frame, had any electrics installed in the doors on a separate line, then met back up with the same space frame later down the line to be bolted on. Thats why every Fiero door had a VIN sticker on it, to make sure it meets back up with the correct space frame.<P>Figure I will mention, I added PW, PL, and PM in my Formulas original doors. It wasn't really all that difficult. Just make sure to get ALL of the wiring harness from donor car(s).<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 03-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||286SE'S||03-19-2001||03:38 PM||||After reading the above responses, I see that your question was not answered completely.<BR>The window/lock harness is one piece. There are relays mounted over the front passenger footwell for the locks. The lock relays must be grounded to operate so intall them in the same place. The wiring is somewhat integrated to the interior lights but you can ignore those wires. Since it is a big harness, you will have to remove the center console sections and dashboard to get all of that out of the doner car. The windows do not use any relays but have circuit breakers in the fuse box. Power mirrors have their own harness. ||||reg||1
